pragma cashscript ^0.13.0;

/**
 * eSahod — PayrollTreasury  (PRIVATE SECTOR)
 * ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
 *
 * One company's payroll funding, held on chain for one funding cycle.
 *
 * The contract holds ePHP fungible CashTokens (1 unit = 1 centavo) and will
 * release them in exactly one shape: a lawful semi-monthly payroll
 * disbursement for one employee.
 *
 *   net pay     -> the employee named inside the Employment NFT commitment
 *   SSS         -> 15% of the bracketed Monthly Salary Credit, / 2, + EC premium
 *   PhilHealth  ->  5% of basic salary, / 2   (floor P10,000, ceiling P100,000)
 *   Pag-IBIG    ->  4% of capped compensation, / 2   (MFS cap P10,000)
 *   BIR         -> the withholding tax HR stored in the NFT
 *
 * WHY THIS EXISTS AT ALL. In a normal payroll the deduction and the remittance
 * are two different events months apart: money is withheld from the worker in
 * January and reaches SSS in April, if it reaches SSS at all. Non-remittance of
 * withheld contributions is the single most common wage complaint in the
 * Philippines, and it is invisible to the worker until they file a claim.
 * Here they are the SAME EVENT — one transaction, atomic. If the employee is
 * paid, the funds have already moved. There is no window in which an employer
 * holds withheld money.
 *
 * WHO CAN RUN IT: anybody. `paySalary` requires NO signature. The employee, the
 * payroll officer, or an anonymous keeper bot can broadcast it and the result
 * is byte-for-byte identical, because every recipient and every amount is
 * derived from the Employment NFT and the statutory formulas below. Nobody —
 * not the officer, not HR, not the developers — can redirect one centavo.
 * That is what makes it safe to give the trigger away, and giving the trigger
 * away is what makes the payroll unstoppable: an employer cannot withhold pay
 * by simply not pressing the button.
 *
 * ── THE TRUST ROOT, STATED HONESTLY ───────────────────────────────────────
 * This covenant trusts the Employment NFT CATEGORY, and nothing above it.
 * Whoever holds the category's minting NFT — HR — can mint a record carrying
 * ANY payeePkh, so a compromised HR minting key can direct funds to a forged
 * employment record through a perfectly lawful-looking payroll run. That is
 * the deliberate design boundary, not an oversight. Pinning the vault's
 * address here would not close it: a forged record minted INTO the vault is
 * paid exactly like a genuine one, because this covenant cannot tell a
 * genuine record of the trusted category from a forged one — see the second
 * CONTRAST case in `tests/infrastructure/esahod/genesis.test.ts`, which
 * drains 60% of a treasury that way. (Pinning is also awkward in practice:
 * the vault must be deployed knowing `treasuryLock`, so the treasury cannot
 * be deployed knowing `vaultLock`.) What the covenant DOES guarantee is that
 * every record of the trusted category is paid exactly by the statutes below
 * — the mint is the only doorway, and hardening it is operational, not
 * script-level: `src/infrastructure/blockchain/esahod/genesis.ts` mints every
 * employment record straight into the vault and destroys the category's
 * minting authority in the SAME transaction, so after genesis nobody — not
 * HR, not a thief who steals HR's key — can ever mint another record of this
 * category again. See that file's comment for why "keep the minting NFT
 * around for future hires" (an earlier draft of this project's tooling did
 * exactly that) reopens this hole, and `tests/infrastructure/esahod/
 * genesis.test.ts` for the forged-mint attack shown working when the baton
 * survives, and failing to even exist once it doesn't. If new hires must be
 * enrollable after genesis, a baton covenant must constrain WHAT is minted
 * (a commitment pre-committed by a separate key, or co-signed by the
 * employee) — constraining only where the NFT lands is not enough, as that
 * same test file demonstrates.
 *
 * ── PRIVATE SECTOR, NOT GOVERNMENT ────────────────────────────────────────
 * SSS (RA 11199) replaces GSIS. The difference is structural, not cosmetic:
 * GSIS is a flat percentage of actual compensation with no ceiling, while SSS
 * is computed on a BRACKETED Monthly Salary Credit in P500 steps, floored at
 * P5,000 and capped at P35,000, plus a flat employer-only EC premium. Two
 * employees earning P16,100 and P16,300 contribute the same amount. That
 * bracket step is reproduced below in the same expression form as
 * `src/domain/statutory/deductions.ts`, because the off-chain calculator and
 * this covenant must agree to the centavo or the transaction is rejected.
 *
 * Statutory basis:
 *   SSS         RA 11199   15% of MSC (5% EE / 10% ER), MSC P5,000–P35,000,
 *                          EC premium P10/month, P30/month at MSC >= P15,000
 *   PhilHealth  RA 11223   5% of basic, split evenly, floor P10,000, ceiling P100,000
 *   Pag-IBIG    RA 9679    2% EE (1% at or below P1,500) + 2% ER, MFS cap P10,000
 *   BIR         RA 10963   withholding computed off chain by HR — see below
 *
 * WHERE THE CHAIN STOPS. This covenant does NOT compute withholding tax, and
 * that is deliberate. Real withholding is annualised, depends on substituted
 * filing, year-to-date compensation and the taxability of individual
 * allowances, and minimum-wage earners are exempt outright under RA 9504.
 * None of that is verifiable from a 40-byte commitment. HR computes the number
 * and writes it into the employment record; the chain enforces that EXACT
 * number reaches EXACTLY the BIR address. Enforce what is verifiable, refuse
 * to pretend about the rest.
 *
 * ── NOTES FOR THE TRANSACTION BUILDER ─────────────────────────────────────
 * 1. `tx.time` compiles to OP_CHECKLOCKTIMEVERIFY, which BCH consensus only
 *    enforces when the spending input carries a non-final sequence number. The
 *    builder must set nLockTime AND a sequence number below 0xffffffff, or the
 *    payday floor is not enforced at all.
 * 2. This script is 533 bytes and 284 opcodes. It therefore requires the May
 *    2025 VM-limits upgrade, which replaced the old 201-opcode ceiling and the
 *    520-byte stack-item cap with density-based limits (items up to 10,000
 *    bytes). The two satoshi-floor checks are what pushed it past the historic
 *    520-byte push limit; keeping the treasury's BCH un-strippable is worth
 *    more than staying quaint under a rule the network no longer enforces.
 */
contract PayrollTreasury(
    bytes32 employmentCategory,  // CashToken category of this company's Employment NFTs — the trust root
    bytes32 pesoCategory,        // CashToken category of ePHP (1 unit = 1 centavo)
    bytes20 remitConfigHash,     // hash160(sssPkh + phicPkh + hdmfPkh + birPkh)
    int     genesisTime,         // unix seconds: earliest claimable moment of period 0
    int     periodSeconds,       // 1314873 = one semi-monthly period (31556952 / 24)
    bytes20 payrollOfficerPkh,   // company payroll officer / treasurer (make this a multisig in production)
    int     lapseTime            // unix seconds: this funding cycle ends
) {
    /**
     * Pay one employee for one semi-monthly period. Permissionless.
     *
     * The four remittance hashes are passed as arguments and checked against a
     * single stored hash160. Storing one hash instead of four addresses keeps
     * the contract parameters small while still letting the company publish its
     * remittance configuration and let anyone verify it — and it means changing
     * a remittance address changes the treasury address, which is exactly the
     * blast radius you want for a change of that severity.
     *
     * Required transaction shape when tax > 0:
     *   in  0  this treasury             out 0  net pay -> employee
     *   in  1  Employment NFT (mutable)  out 1  SSS
     *   in  2+ fee UTXOs (optional)      out 2  PhilHealth
     *                                    out 3  Pag-IBIG
     *                                    out 4  BIR
     *                                    out 5  Employment NFT, period + 1
     *                                    out 6  treasury change
     *                                    out 7+ BCH change (optional)
     *
     * When tax == 0 the BIR output is OMITTED — a zero-amount CashToken output
     * is invalid under consensus — and everything after it shifts up one slot:
     *   in  0  this treasury             out 0  net pay -> employee
     *   in  1  Employment NFT (mutable)  out 1  SSS
     *   in  2+ fee UTXOs (optional)      out 2  PhilHealth
     *                                    out 3  Pag-IBIG
     *                                    out 4  Employment NFT, period + 1
     *                                    out 5  treasury change
     *                                    out 6+ BCH change (optional)
     */
    function paySalary(
        bytes20 sssPkh,
        bytes20 phicPkh,
        bytes20 hdmfPkh,
        bytes20 birPkh
    ) {
        // ── 0. Pin the transaction shape ─────────────────────────────────
        // Every input runs its own copy of this script. Pinning the treasury to
        // input 0 is what stops a second treasury coin from riding along in the
        // same transaction: input 2 spending this same contract would have to
        // satisfy `activeInputIndex == 0` too, and cannot. One payroll
        // transaction therefore draws from exactly one treasury coin, and the
        // change arithmetic at the bottom accounts for all of it.
        require(this.activeInputIndex == 0, "treasury must be input 0");

        // There is deliberately no "input 0 must hold ePHP" check here: the
        // change clause at the bottom already makes anything else unspendable
        // through this path. A plain BCH coin has a token amount of zero, so the
        // required change would be negative; a coin of some other category would
        // have to be matched centavo for centavo out of the spender's own ePHP.
        // Redundant checks are not free — they are script bytes.

        // THE TRUST ROOT. `employmentCategory + 0x01` means "an NFT of this
        // company's category, with the MUTABLE capability". The category is
        // fixed at deployment and can only ever have been minted by whoever
        // controlled the genesis UTXO, so a forged employment record is
        // impossible: an attacker can mint NFTs all day, but not of this
        // category. The mutable capability is what allows the commitment to be
        // rewritten with the advanced period — an immutable NFT could not
        // advance, and a minting NFT could spawn new employment records.
        require(tx.inputs[1].tokenCategory == employmentCategory + 0x01,
                "input 1 must be a mutable Employment NFT of this company");

        require(hash160(sssPkh + phicPkh + hdmfPkh + birPkh) == remitConfigHash,
                "remittance configuration does not match");

        // ── 1. Read the employment record out of the 40-byte commitment ──
        //  [0:20] payeePkh   [20:24] monthlyBasic  [24:28] monthlyAllowance
        //  [28:32] taxPerPeriod  [32:34] nextPeriod  [34:36] endPeriod
        //  [36:37] status  [37:40] employeeNo
        //
        // The payee lives INSIDE the commitment rather than in the contract
        // parameters. That is the single design decision that makes triggering
        // payroll safe to hand to a stranger: the trigger carries no discretion.
        bytes payeePkh, bytes c1 = tx.inputs[1].nftCommitment.split(20);
        bytes basicB,   bytes c2 = c1.split(4);
        bytes allowB,   bytes c3 = c2.split(4);
        bytes taxB,     bytes c4 = c3.split(4);
        bytes periodB,  bytes c5 = c4.split(2);
        bytes endB,     bytes c6 = c5.split(2);
        bytes statusB,  bytes employeeNoB = c6.split(1);

        int monthlyBasic     = int(basicB);
        int monthlyAllowance = int(allowB);   // allowances forming part of compensation
        int tax              = int(taxB);
        int period           = int(periodB);

        // Suspension and separation are a status flag, not a key rotation:
        // flipping this byte to 0 stops payroll for this employee immediately
        // and permanently, without touching anyone else's record.
        require(int(statusB) == 1, "employee is not in active status");

        // Fixed-term and project-based contracts expire on their own, with no
        // action required from anyone. This is the clause that stops a payroll
        // covenant from paying a separated employee forever.
        require(period <= int(endB), "employment record has ended");

        // ── 2. The payday floor ──────────────────────────────────────────
        // OP_CHECKLOCKTIMEVERIFY. The contract guarantees "never earlier than
        // payday". The employee's own ability to broadcast guarantees "never
        // much later" — they do not need the employer's cooperation to be paid,
        // which is the whole point. The counter in the commitment is what makes
        // this safe: period N can only be claimed once, because claiming it
        // rewrites the record to period N+1.
        require(tx.time >= genesisTime + period * periodSeconds,
                "payday for this period has not arrived");

        // ── 3. Statutory arithmetic — every value in centavos ────────────
        //
        // READ THIS BEFORE "SIMPLIFYING" ANYTHING BELOW. Integer division
        // truncates. `x * 5 / 200` is NOT `x * 5 / 100 / 2` — the second
        // truncates twice and drifts by a centavo, and a one-centavo drift
        // makes the transaction unspendable with an error message that tells
        // you nothing. Every expression here is character-for-character the
        // same as `src/domain/statutory/deductions.ts`. Change them together
        // or not at all.
        int monthlyCompensation = monthlyBasic + monthlyAllowance;

        // ── SSS (RA 11199) ── The Monthly Salary Credit is a BRACKET, not a
        // salary. SSS publishes a table in P500 steps: everything from
        // P5,250 to P5,749.99 maps to an MSC of P5,500. Adding half a step
        // before truncating is round-half-up, which reproduces the published
        // table exactly in three operations.
        int msc = ((monthlyCompensation + 25000) / 50000) * 50000;
        if (msc < 500000)  { msc = 500000;  }   // MSC floor    P5,000
        if (msc > 3500000) { msc = 3500000; }   // MSC ceiling  P35,000 — the contribution caps here

        // 15% of the MSC, split 5% employee / 10% employer, and half of the
        // monthly amount falls due each semi-monthly cut-off: rate/100 / 2.
        int sssEE = msc * 5  / 200;
        int sssER = msc * 10 / 200;

        // Employees' Compensation premium: employer-only, and a flat peso
        // amount rather than a rate — P10/month below MSC P15,000, P30/month at
        // or above it. Halved for the cut-off, so 500 or 1500 centavos.
        int sssEC = 500;
        if (msc >= 1500000) { sssEC = 1500; }

        // One output covers all three. SSS receives the employee share, the
        // employer share and the EC premium in a single payment, which is how
        // it is actually remitted on the R-5.
        int sssTotal = sssEE + sssER + sssEC;

        // ── PhilHealth (RA 11223) ── BASIC salary only. Allowances are
        // excluded by law, which is why this base is computed separately from
        // the SSS one instead of reusing it.
        int phicBase = monthlyBasic;
        if (phicBase < 1000000)  { phicBase = 1000000;  }   // income floor   P10,000
        if (phicBase > 10000000) { phicBase = 10000000; }   // income ceiling P100,000

        // 5% total premium: 50/1000 * 1/2 (employee share) * 1/2 (cut-off)
        // == 50/4000, in one division so it truncates exactly once.
        int phicEE = phicBase * 50 / 4000;
        int phicTotal = phicEE * 2;   // employer matches the employee peso for peso

        // ── Pag-IBIG (RA 9679, HDMF Circular 460) ── total compensation,
        // capped at the Maximum Fund Salary. The cap binds for almost every
        // employee, which is why both fixtures land on the same P100 figure.
        int hdmfBase = monthlyCompensation;
        if (hdmfBase > 1000000) { hdmfBase = 1000000; }   // MFS P10,000

        // The employee rate — and ONLY the employee rate — drops to 1% at or
        // below P1,500 of monthly compensation. The employer always pays 2%.
        // Note the rate test uses uncapped compensation while the base is
        // capped; they are different quantities and swapping them is a
        // classic payroll bug.
        int hdmfEeRate = 2;
        if (monthlyCompensation <= 150000) { hdmfEeRate = 1; }

        int hdmfEE = hdmfBase * hdmfEeRate / 200;
        int hdmfER = hdmfBase * 2 / 200;
        int hdmfTotal = hdmfEE + hdmfER;

        // ── Net pay ── gross for the cut-off is half of monthly compensation;
        // the employee's own three shares and the withholding tax come off it.
        // The employer shares never touch this line — they are additional cost,
        // not a deduction, and conflating the two is how workers get shorted.
        int gross = monthlyCompensation / 2;
        int net = gross - sssEE - phicEE - hdmfEE - tax;

        // A non-positive net means the record is misconfigured (a tax figure
        // larger than the pay, say). Fail loudly rather than mint an invalid
        // zero-value token output further down.
        require(net > 0, "computed net pay is not positive");

        // ── 4. Enforce the disbursement, output by output ────────────────
        // For each output all three of lockingBytecode, tokenCategory and
        // tokenAmount are pinned. Pinning only two is the classic covenant
        // hole: the right amount of the WRONG TOKEN, or the right token to the
        // wrong address, would both still satisfy a partial check.
        require(tx.outputs[0].lockingBytecode == new LockingBytecodeP2PKH(unsafe_bytes20(payeePkh)),
                "output 0 must pay the employee named in the record");
        require(tx.outputs[0].tokenCategory == pesoCategory, "output 0 must be ePHP");
        require(tx.outputs[0].tokenAmount == net, "output 0 must be exactly net pay");

        require(tx.outputs[1].lockingBytecode == new LockingBytecodeP2PKH(sssPkh),
                "output 1 must pay SSS");
        require(tx.outputs[1].tokenCategory == pesoCategory, "output 1 must be ePHP");
        require(tx.outputs[1].tokenAmount == sssTotal, "output 1 must be the exact SSS contribution");

        require(tx.outputs[2].lockingBytecode == new LockingBytecodeP2PKH(phicPkh),
                "output 2 must pay PhilHealth");
        require(tx.outputs[2].tokenCategory == pesoCategory, "output 2 must be ePHP");
        require(tx.outputs[2].tokenAmount == phicTotal, "output 2 must be the exact PhilHealth premium");

        require(tx.outputs[3].lockingBytecode == new LockingBytecodeP2PKH(hdmfPkh),
                "output 3 must pay Pag-IBIG");
        require(tx.outputs[3].tokenCategory == pesoCategory, "output 3 must be ePHP");
        require(tx.outputs[3].tokenAmount == hdmfTotal, "output 3 must be the exact Pag-IBIG contribution");

        // ── The BIR output disappears when there is no tax ────────────────
        // A CashToken output carrying zero fungible units is INVALID under
        // consensus, so a zero-tax payroll cannot simply send 0 to the BIR —
        // the output must not exist, and everything after it shifts up by one.
        // Minimum-wage earners are exempt from income tax under RA 9504, so in
        // a Philippine private payroll this is the COMMON case, not the edge
        // case. Getting it wrong makes the covenant unusable for exactly the
        // workers it most needs to protect.
        int nftOut    = 5;
        int changeOut = 6;
        if (tax > 0) {
            require(tx.outputs[4].lockingBytecode == new LockingBytecodeP2PKH(birPkh),
                    "output 4 must pay the BIR");
            require(tx.outputs[4].tokenCategory == pesoCategory, "output 4 must be ePHP");
            require(tx.outputs[4].tokenAmount == tax, "output 4 must be the exact withholding tax");
        } else {
            nftOut    = 4;
            changeOut = 5;
        }

        // ── 5. Return the employment record, period advanced by one ──────
        // The commitment is rebuilt from the ORIGINAL byte slices with only
        // nextPeriod replaced. Not "checked for equality field by field" —
        // rebuilt, so there is no field anyone forgot to check. A payroll run
        // therefore cannot change a salary, a payee, an end date or an
        // employment status, no matter who broadcasts it.
        //
        // Sending the NFT back to the same lockingBytecode keeps it in the
        // EmploymentVault, whose `payroll()` path in turn requires input 0 to
        // be THIS treasury. Neither contract can be paired with a look-alike:
        // the treasury insists on a real employment record, and the vault
        // insists on a real treasury.
        require(tx.outputs[nftOut].lockingBytecode == tx.inputs[1].lockingBytecode,
                "the employment record must return to its vault");
        require(tx.outputs[nftOut].tokenCategory == tx.inputs[1].tokenCategory,
                "the employment record must keep its category and mutable capability");
        require(tx.outputs[nftOut].nftCommitment ==
                    payeePkh + basicB + allowB + taxB
                    + toPaddedBytes(period + 1, 2)
                    + endB + statusB + employeeNoB,
                "only nextPeriod may change, and only by +1");

        // THE SATOSHI FLOOR, part one. Token checks alone say nothing about
        // the BCH riding under them. Without this line the anonymous
        // broadcaster this design deliberately invites could return the NFT
        // with a dust-minimum 546 sats and pocket the difference in their own
        // change output — a lawful payroll run doubling as a slow drain of the
        // record's carrying value. The NFT's satoshis must ride along
        // undiminished; the separate fee input (in 2+) pays for dust and fees.
        require(tx.outputs[nftOut].value >= tx.inputs[1].value,
                "the employment record must keep its satoshis");

        // ── 6. Re-create the treasury with the exact remainder ───────────
        // THE ANTI-LEAK CLAUSE. Every centavo of the input that was not
        // disbursed above must come back to this same contract address. There
        // is no "and the rest goes wherever" branch: an extra peso output for
        // anybody else would have to be funded from the transaction's own
        // inputs, never from the payroll funding.
        //
        // Note this cannot produce a zero-value change output — the treasury
        // may not be drained to exactly zero, since a zero-amount token output
        // is invalid. The builder leaves a remainder; the final sweep uses
        // `sweepLapsedNca`.
        require(tx.outputs[changeOut].lockingBytecode == tx.inputs[0].lockingBytecode,
                "the remaining payroll funding must return to the treasury");
        require(tx.outputs[changeOut].tokenCategory == pesoCategory,
                "treasury change must be ePHP");
        require(tx.outputs[changeOut].tokenAmount ==
                    tx.inputs[0].tokenAmount - net - sssTotal - phicTotal - hdmfTotal - tax,
                "treasury change must be the exact remainder");

        // THE SATOSHI FLOOR, part two — the big one. The clauses above pin
        // every output's lockingBytecode, tokenCategory and tokenAmount, but a
        // token covenant that never mentions `.value` has still left its BCH
        // unguarded: the broadcaster could rebuild this change output with the
        // exact ePHP remainder sitting on 1,000 sats and strip the treasury
        // coin's entire BCH balance into their own output. Requiring the
        // change to carry at least the input's satoshis closes that: the
        // treasury's BCH rides along undiminished, and the fee input funds
        // the dust on every disbursement output plus the mining fee.
        require(tx.outputs[changeOut].value >= tx.inputs[0].value,
                "the treasury must keep its satoshis");
    }

    /**
     * Close out the funding cycle.
     *
     * Payroll funding is allocated per cycle. After `lapseTime` the payroll
     * officer returns whatever is left to the company's operating account and a
     * fresh treasury is deployed for the next cycle. Before `lapseTime` this
     * path is dead: the officer cannot recall funds an employee is still owed,
     * which is the difference between an escrow and a promise.
     *
     * `tx.inputs.length == 1` is not a formality. Employment NFTs live in a
     * contract that unlocks whenever input 0 is this treasury, so without this
     * clause a lapsed-cycle sweep could drag employment records into the same
     * transaction and rewrite them — handing the officer a power that belongs
     * exclusively to HR. Restricting the sweep to a lone input closes that
     * completely, at the cost of needing one transaction per treasury coin and
     * paying its fee from the coin's own satoshis.
     */
    function sweepLapsedNca(sig officerSig, pubkey officerPk) {
        require(hash160(officerPk) == payrollOfficerPkh, "not the payroll officer");
        require(checkSig(officerSig, officerPk), "invalid payroll officer signature");
        require(tx.time >= lapseTime, "this payroll funding cycle has not lapsed yet");
        require(tx.inputs.length == 1, "a lapse sweep may not co-spend any other input");
    }
}
